# LINE ENDINGS, PINNED.
#
# core.autocrlf is on for at least one developer here, which meant files were
# stored as LF and checked out as CRLF on Windows and LF on Linux. That is
# usually invisible — until something compares file contents byte for byte.
#
# tests/goldens/* are exactly that: a recorded response compared against a fresh
# one. A golden recorded on Windows and compared on Linux differed on every
# line, starting with `{` against `{\r`, which is a diff that says nothing about
# the response and everything about the checkout. The comparison strips line
# endings now, and this pins the stored form too, so the two halves agree from
# either direction.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Goldens deliberately take the rule above rather than an exemption. Marking
# them -text ("never convert") would have stored whatever bytes the machine
# doing the commit happened to hold, pinning the fixtures to one checkout
# instead of to LF -- which is the bug, not the fix.

# Binary, and none of git's business.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
